00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.760 align:start position:0%
 
mr. vice president thank you so much for  joining us and our socially distant  manner here good to see you lore  dr. Fauci today told The Associated  Press that quote we're not there yet in  terms of tracing and tracking that he  believes is necessary in order to reopen  the country what does that mean exactly  Americans I think are looking for both  safety and some specifics here we really  are and president of trumps made it very  clear that he wants to reopen the  American economy but if we want to do it  at the time that is responsible can be  safe for the American people and we're  going to be producing new guidance for  states across the country too to know  when that will be possible but but as  dr. Faust you suggest a big part of that  will be the kind of testing not only  testing where you can diagnose whether  or not you have the corona virus but  also what the experts call the kind of  surveillance testing and literally just  this morning we we brought our entire  testing team together US Public Health  Service dr. Burks all of our supply  personnel and we're developing a  framework as we speak for making it  possible for a broad range of resources  to be available across the country the  concern is of course for Public Safety  but also whether these goals being set  by the healthcare professionals that  look at this very important aspect of  American life obviously that by some  measure six and seven million tests a  week and some by some measures per day  would be necessary in order to reach  that you know ultimate testing and  tracking a bar is that really realistic  and can the country wait until we have  all the people in place all the  volunteers in place all the framework in  place in order to do that well that will  be a decision for the President of the  United States to make and and right now  the president as you saw today unveiled  a broad range of leaders from virtually  every sector of the economy that will be  giving him counsel about about how we  can move forward in a responsible way to  reopen our economy  but make no mistake about it we're gonna  listen very carefully to our healthcare  experts and ensure that we have a  sufficient amount of both the diagnostic  and the surveillance testing to be able  to allow many businesses to begin to  reopen may be educational institutions  reopen but but our recommendations will  be specific to those areas of the  country where we've seen some limited  impact but we'll also have guidance for  areas of the country that have seen a  large outbreak of the corona body he  said but it's it's pretty much really  optimistic that think May 1st is going  to be possible that those are all the  signs he's giving in multiple interviews  my question is is there any is there any  dispute between kind of a more  pessimistic which he says I'm kind of  always pessimistic about this cautiously  optimistic and the president given the  real pain also being felt by those  who've lost their jobs and a lot of  people losing hope out there that this  is ever going to reopen well let me let  me say that the timing will all be  determined by the president when we're  at a responsible moment where we are on  track to put the coronavirus in the past  and that's in the hands of the American  people on the testing and tracking that  we're hearing about there are a lot of  Americans I think stuff they read online  and so they're the government's gonna  track me that government's not going to  let me work unless I'm tracked and  tested and and so forth what can you  tell the American people about that  given what they've heard about some of  these solutions in order to reopen will  always protect the privacy and the  liberty of the American people and the  testing recommendations that will  accompany new guidelines for reopening  America will make it possible for us to  do two things Laura number one is to be  able to quickly diagnose people that  have symptoms that may be from  coronavirus but when you hear about  surveillance testing that just simply  means being able to test a sufficient  number of people  to determine those that may have the  coronavirus but not be aware of it but  it's simply two different models of  testing and making sure that we deploy  the resources and activate resources  around the country to do both things  will be an important part of not only  reopening America but keeping America  open how close are we on the immunity  testing though because I know it's a  little iffy on whether false positives  false negatives and whether they are  necessarily that effective in  determining whether you've actually been  exposed or just may have been exposed we  expect the FDA to approve a new antibody  test in a matter of days and and if  approved could literally create more  than 20 million new tests a month and  but to your point Laura that'll be a  test that will tell you whether you have  had the corona virus in the past and  that whether you might in fact be immune  to it going forward andrew Cuomo said  today and his daily press conference  also an appearance on TV that if the  White House tried to force New York to  reopen he would take the White House to  court is that the state of affairs the  relationship between the White House and  New York at this point we have forged a  very strong working relationship with  Governor Cuomo with mayor de Blasio on  behalf of all of the people of New York  and and I have every confidence that  going forward as the president makes a  decision and we issue new guidelines for  reopening America that that not only New  York but states around the country will  continue to cooperate will continue to  work with us because we'll be tailoring  those guidelines based upon the science  based upon the best interest and based  upon the corona virus outbreak in those  particular communities and so we'll  there will always be disagreements in  public life but but I have to tell you  where we've come to today and I know  where we'll go tomorrow the American  people can be confident President Donald  Trump's going to continue to marshal all  the resources of the federal government  will partner with our state and local  governments and will always put the  health of America first it's not a one  size fits all for the whole country New  York is different from Nebraska it's  it's not a one size fits all I think one  of the remarkable things about the team  that the president assembled was our  health experts have literally been  tracking on a county-by-county basis the  outbreak of the corona virus and and  we've been working with States and I can  tell you that at the president's  direction we've ensured that states have  what they need when they need it based  upon the dimensions of the outbreak in  that state when we issue the guidance  we'll focus the guidance based upon what  what the data and what the science and  what the best recommendations in America  show into account the other fallout  social isolation depression suicide drug  abuse spousal abuse child abuse  despondency we saw that after the 2008  collapse now the IMF is saying this  downturn could be the worst since the  Great Depression so are you taking that  into account while you look at all of  the medical data underlying the virus  itself president Trump has said this is  clearly going to be the biggest decision  of his life and it's because he's  looking at every aspect of this and when  he speaks about his unique  responsibilities as president it's  because he understands that it's not  just a matter of looking at health data  it's a matter of looking at what's in  the interest of our economy what's in  the in the interest as you rightly  pretend the emotional and psychological  impact on Americans and and he's brought  it he's brought advisers together to  bring the full range of counsel to him  to make that decision vice president  pence the Senate is out until at least  the next seven days out of not coming  back to Washington the house isn't going  to come back until May fourth you have  spoken so eloquently about the whole of  government approach is that the whole of  government when we're in the middle of  what you and the president and  everybody's describing as a war against  this health crisis and this virus I  think should Congress be part of this  decision about whether we're going to  open or cleat you stay closed and if not  why not well I think with regard to the  decision about reopening America what  the president announced today is we've  brought together not just business  leaders from every sector of our economy  but political leaders in both political  parties to offer counsel so you're fine  with them not going back into session  during this the biggest crisis we've had  and they're in their home district I  mean you obviously served and served on  the hill and you know what it was doing  so but government workers are putting  their lives on the line doctors and  everyone else and and we're spending  trillions of dollars and yet they fly in  to vote and then they leave or I take  two things about that number one we're  very grateful for the bipartisan efforts  that have put three bills on the desk  that not only you know made corona virus  testing free for every American extended  paid family leave but the extraordinary  rollout over the last week of paycheck  protection allowing small businesses to  keep people on the payroll for two  months support for industries around the  country it really has been Congress at  its best in passing all these bills but  but to your point remember Congress can  act by what's called unanimous consent  and we're really calling on the leaders  of the Congress to expand the paycheck  protection bill another 250 billion  dollars there's been such tremendous  interest in  that that Congress can act by a voice  vote and we're encouraging them to do  that even even without having to come  back to Washington DC

